    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's an interesting distinction. so if the disease itself is just more aggressive in those groups, does that mean we should basically expect different biological drivers or genetic predispositions that make it worse? like, is there a fundamental biological difference in how melanoma behaves in non-white patients compared to the \"baseline\" or \"standard\" white patient, or is it just a matter of timing and detection? i'm trying to understand if there's a way to categorize these different 'types' of melanoma based on the patient's race.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
